@node dd___Raw_block_transfer_tool "dd - Raw block transfer tool"
@{b}@{u}dd - Raw block transfer tool@{uu}@{ub}

@{fg shine}dd@{fg text} reads and writes raw disk blocks. Since dd 2.0 it uses the AmigaOS
argument parser. Type @{fg shine}dd ?@{fg text} (or @{fg shine}dd HELP@{fg text} ) to see the quick reference.

@{i}Template@{ui}

@{fg highlight}
  SRC,DST,UNIT/N,START/N,COUNT/N,BS/N,US=UNITSRC/N/K,UD=UNITDST/N/K,HELP=H/S,INSPECT=I/K,VERBOSE=V/S,CMD/K,CS=CMDSRC/K,CD=CMDDST/K,MT=MAXTRANSFER/N/K,MEM/K
@{fg text}

@{i}Positional form@{ui}

@{fg highlight}
  dd <src> <dst> [<unit>] [<start>] [<count>] [<blocksize>]
@{fg text}

@{fg shine}SRC@{fg text} and @{fg shine}DST@{fg text} are required. The single @{fg shine}UNIT@{fg text} slot applies to whichever of
@{fg shine}SRC@{fg text} / @{fg shine}DST@{fg text} is a @{fg shine}.device@{fg text} . If @{b}both@{ub} are devices, use the keyword form
@{fg shine}US <n>@{fg text} / @{fg shine}UD <n>@{fg text} to disambiguate.

@{i}Special names@{ui}

  @{b}Name@{ub}      @{b}Where@{ub}  @{b}Meaning@{ub}                   
  --------  -----  ------------------------  
  @{fg shine}FILL:@{fg text}     SRC    Infinite stream of zero   
                   bytes. @{fg shine}FILL:255@{fg text} ,         
                   @{fg shine}FILL:170@{fg text} , ... for other  
                   byte values.              
  @{fg shine}RSPEED:@{fg text}   DST    Read-only throughput      
                   benchmark (no actual      
                   write).                   
  @{fg shine}RWSPEED:@{fg text}  DST    Read+write throughput     
                   benchmark.                

dd transfers whole blocks. When the source is a file whose size is not a
multiple of the block size, the destination's last block is read back
first and only the part the file covers is replaced, so the bytes past
the end of the file keep the content they had:

@{fg highlight}
  note: source file ends mid-block, keeping the 24 bytes already there
@{fg text}

The read-back happens before anything is written: if the destination
cannot hand that block over, dd reports it and transfers nothing.

A file destination takes any byte count, so there dd writes what the
source had and the copy comes out the same size.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Main}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link INSPECT_mode}

@endnode

@node INSPECT_mode "INSPECT mode"
@{b}INSPECT mode@{ub}

@{fg shine}dd INSPECT <device.name> [UNIT n] [VERBOSE]@{fg text} (short: @{fg shine}dd I <device.name>@{fg text} )
probes the device and prints what dd sees, without doing any I/O. Add
@{fg shine}VERBOSE@{fg text} to also list the driver's full @{fg shine}SupportedCommands@{fg text} set:

@{fg highlight}
  dd INSPECT compactflash.device UNIT 0
  dd INSPECT scsi.device VERBOSE
@{fg text}

Example output (@{fg shine}VERBOSE@{fg text}, so the command list is included):

@{fg highlight}
  scsi.device unit 0:
    sector size:    512
    total sectors:  7831152
    cylinders:      31076
    heads:          4
    sec/track:      63
    buf mem type:   $00000001
    device type:    0, flags: $00
    >4GiB methods:  NSCMD_TD64, TD64, HD_SCSI
    commands:
                    $0002 (CMD_READ)
                    $0003 (CMD_WRITE)
                    $0016 (TD_GETGEOMETRY)
                    $0018 (TD_READ64)
                    $0019 (TD_WRITE64)
                    $001C (HD_SCSICMD)
                    $4000 (NSCMD_DEVICEQUERY)
                    $544A (unknown)
                    $C000 (NSCMD_TD_READ64)
                    $C001 (NSCMD_TD_WRITE64)
    read/write via: NSCMD_TD_READ64 / NSCMD_TD_WRITE64
@{fg text}
(The command list above is abridged)

The output covers:

  * Sector size, total sectors, CHS geometry (cylinders/heads/sec-per-track).
  * @{fg shine}buf mem type:@{fg text} is the memory type the driver asks its buffers to live in (
    @{fg shine}TD_GETGEOMETRY@{fg text} 's @{fg shine}dg_BufMemType@{fg text} ), and @{fg shine}device type:@{fg text} / @{fg shine}flags:@{fg text} are the remaining
    geometry fields. @{fg shine}$00000001@{fg text} is plain @{fg shine}MEMF_PUBLIC@{fg text} , i.e. the driver states no
    DMA restriction. See @{"Transfer size and buffer memory" link transfer_size_and_buffer_memory} .
  * Transfer capabilities: a @{fg shine}>4GiB methods:@{fg text} line summarises the large-transfer
    commands the device offers ( @{fg shine}NSCMD_TD64@{fg text} , @{fg shine}TD64@{fg text} , @{fg shine}HD_SCSI@{fg text} , or @{fg shine}(none)@{fg text} ), and
    with @{fg shine}VERBOSE@{fg text} a @{fg shine}commands:@{fg text} block lists its full command set. Each command is
    shown as its hex value and name, with @{fg shine}(unknown)@{fg text} for any value dd doesn't
    recognise.
  * @{fg shine}read/write via:@{fg text} is the command pair dd would use on this device. A 64-bit
    command the driver advertises is taken whatever the transfer size; when it
    advertises none, the line shows what a whole-device transfer would use. See @{"IO command selection" link io_command_selection} .

Every real transfer also prints a one-line "read: ... via <cmd>" and
"write: ... via <cmd>" before the data loop starts, so you can confirm
the dispatched command without re-running INSPECT, followed by an "xfer:
... bytes per request" line showing the request size in use, with the
memory type named after it when @{fg shine}MEM=@{fg text} asked for one. If a command is
rejected mid-transfer, a "<cmd> not supported, falling back to <cmd>"
line is printed and the swath is retried with the next command down the
ladder.

If a driver reports fewer bytes moved than dd asked for, dd prints
"note: <device> reported N of M bytes moved" once and carries on. Not
every driver fills that field in, so a @{fg shine}0 of M@{fg text} note can also mean the
driver left it at zero.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link dd___Raw_block_transfer_tool}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link IO_command_selection}

@endnode

@node IO_command_selection "IO command selection"
@{b}IO command selection@{ub}
@{i}Subsections:@{ui}
  @{"  Automatic selection  " link Automatic_selection}
  @{"  Forcing the command  " link Forcing_the_command}

For each side of a transfer ( @{fg shine}SRC@{fg text} is the read path, @{fg shine}DST@{fg text} the write path)
dd picks one device command. Files, @{fg shine}FILL:@{fg text} and the speed benchmarks use
plain DOS @{fg shine}Read@{fg text} / @{fg shine}Write@{fg text} instead.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link INSPECT_mode}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Automatic_selection}

@endnode

@node Automatic_selection "Automatic selection"
@{i}Automatic selection@{ui}

The choice depends on what the driver advertises through NSD and, only
when it advertises nothing, on whether the byte range exceeds 32-bit
addressing (4 GiB):

@{fg highlight}
  +---------------------+
  |   real .device ?    |
  +----------+----------+
        no <-+-> yes
         |         |
         v         v
    DOS Read/   +----------------------+
    Write       |  forced SCSI         |
   (file,       |  (bit 7 set) ?       |
    FILL:,      +----------+-----------+
    RSPEED:)          no <-+-> yes
                           |         |
                           v         v
               +--------------------------+  HD_SCSICMD
               | NSD advertises a         |  (block size
               | 64-bit command ?         |   not 2^n)
               +----------+---------------+
                   none <-+-> yes
                     |          |
                     |          +-- NSCMD_TD_READ64 --> NSCMD_TD_READ64
                     |          |                       NSCMD_TD_WRITE64
                     |          +-- TD_READ64 -------->  TD_READ64
                     v                                   TD_WRITE64
          +---------------+                             (any range)
          |  range        |
          |  > 4 GiB ?    |
          +-------+-------+
              no <+-> yes
               |        |
               v        v
          CMD_READ /  TD_READ64      <- tried blind, drops to
          CMD_WRITE   TD_WRITE64        HD_SCSICMD on -3
@{fg text}

A 64-bit command the driver advertises is taken whatever the range, not
only past 4 GiB. It carries the same request as @{fg shine}CMD_READ@{fg text} / @{fg shine}CMD_WRITE@{fg text}
with a zero high offset, so nothing is lost below 4 GiB. The range only
decides when the driver advertises no 64-bit command at all.

If a chosen command is rejected at I/O time with @{fg shine}IOERR_NOCMD@{fg text} (-3), dd
steps one rung down the ladder and retries the @{i}same@{ui} block range,
printing a @{fg shine}<cmd> not supported, falling back to <cmd>@{fg text} notice:

@{fg highlight}
  NSCMD_TD_READ64  -->  TD_READ64  --+
                                     +--->  HD_SCSICMD  --->  report -3
  NSCMD_TD_WRITE64 -->  TD_WRITE64 --+
@{fg text}

@{fg shine}HD_SCSICMD@{fg text} (SCSI @{fg shine}READ10@{fg text} / @{fg shine}WRITE10@{fg text} ) is the last resort -- it needs the
driver to implement raw SCSI passthrough, which not every device
provides. @{fg shine}CMD_READ@{fg text} / @{fg shine}CMD_WRITE@{fg text} and @{fg shine}HD_SCSICMD@{fg text} have no successor, so if
they fail the error is reported. This is why a >4 GiB transfer on a
device with no usable NSD still works: dd starts at @{fg shine}TD_READ64@{fg text} and only
drops to @{fg shine}HD_SCSICMD@{fg text} if the driver rejects TD64 as well.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link IO_command_selection}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Forcing_the_command}

@endnode

@node Forcing_the_command "Forcing the command"
@{i}Forcing the command@{ui}

@{fg shine}CMD=<name>@{fg text} pins the transfer command and skips the selection above
entirely:

  @{b}`CMD=`@{ub}  @{b}Uses@{ub}                                 
  ------  -----------------------------------  
  @{fg shine}AUTO@{fg text}    Auto-detect (default; the diagram +  
          runtime fallback above).             
  @{fg shine}CMD@{fg text}     @{fg shine}CMD_READ@{fg text} / @{fg shine}CMD_WRITE@{fg text}.                
  @{fg shine}TD64@{fg text}    @{fg shine}TD_READ64@{fg text} / @{fg shine}TD_WRITE64@{fg text}.              
  @{fg shine}NSCMD@{fg text}   @{fg shine}NSCMD_TD_READ64@{fg text} / @{fg shine}NSCMD_TD_WRITE64@{fg text}.  
  @{fg shine}SCSI@{fg text}    @{fg shine}HD_SCSICMD@{fg text}.                          

A forced command is honored strictly: it is @{b}not@{ub} auto-downgraded, so if
the device rejects it the error is reported (e.g. @{fg shine}read error (-3)@{fg text} ).
@{fg shine}CMD=CMD@{fg text} uses a 32-bit byte offset and is refused for transfers that
cross 4 GiB (use @{fg shine}TD64@{fg text} / @{fg shine}NSCMD@{fg text} / @{fg shine}SCSI@{fg text} for those). The byte-offset
commands ( @{fg shine}CMD@{fg text} / @{fg shine}TD64@{fg text} / @{fg shine}NSCMD@{fg text} ) assume a power-of-2 block size; for an
odd block size use @{fg shine}CMD=SCSI@{fg text} . @{fg shine}CMD=@{fg text} does not affect INSPECT output, which
reports what the automatic selection would pick.

When the chosen command is one the driver does not advertise through
NSD, dd says so before the transfer starts and lists what the device
does offer:

@{fg highlight}
  dd: warning: myscsi.device unit 0 does not advertise TD64
    >4GiB methods:  NSCMD_TD64, HD_SCSI
@{fg text}

The override is still applied as asked. dd can only make this check when
the driver answers @{fg shine}NSCMD_DEVICEQUERY@{fg text} ; without NSD there is no
capability list to compare against and dd stays quiet.

@{b}Per-side overrides@{ub}

@{fg shine}CMD=@{fg text} sets both sides. @{fg shine}CMDSRC=<name>@{fg text} (short @{fg shine}CS=@{fg text} ) and @{fg shine}CMDDST=<name>@{fg text}
(short @{fg shine}CD=@{fg text} ) set one side each and take precedence over @{fg shine}CMD=@{fg text} for that
side. Use them for a device-to-device copy where the two drivers support
different command families:

@{fg highlight}
  ; TD64 on the read side, NSCMD on the write side
  dd cf.device scsi.device US 0 UD 0 CS TD64 CD NSCMD
@{fg text}

Note that @{fg shine}CMD@{fg text} / @{fg shine}CMDSRC@{fg text} / @{fg shine}CMDDST@{fg text} only affect sides that are a real
@{fg shine}.device@{fg text} . A file, @{fg shine}FILL:@{fg text} , @{fg shine}RSPEED:@{fg text} or @{fg shine}RWSPEED:@{fg text} side always uses DOS @{fg shine}Read@{fg text}
/ @{fg shine}Write@{fg text} , so forcing a command there has no effect at all: in
@{fg shine}dd CD0:IMAGE scsi.device 0 CMD=TD64@{fg text} the only side the override reaches
is the write to @{fg shine}scsi.device@{fg text} .

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Automatic_selection}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Transfer_size_and_buffer_memory}

@endnode

@node Transfer_size_and_buffer_memory "Transfer size and buffer memory"
@{b}Transfer size and buffer memory@{ub}

dd moves data in swaths, one device request per swath. By default a
swath is 130560 bytes (1 MB for the @{fg shine}RSPEED:@{fg text} / @{fg shine}RWSPEED:@{fg text} benchmarks),
allocated from @{fg shine}MEMF_PUBLIC@{fg text} plus whatever memory type the driver asked
for through @{fg shine}dg_BufMemType@{fg text} . Both are adjustable:

  @{b}Argument@{ub}                   @{b}Meaning@{ub}                                 
  -------------------------  --------------------------------------  
  @{fg shine}MAXTRANSFER=<bytes>@{fg text} (@{fg shine}MT=@{fg text})  Bytes per device request. Rounded down  
                             to a whole number of 512-byte units     
                             and clamped to 1 MB.                    
  @{fg shine}MEM=<type>@{fg text}                 Buffer memory: @{fg shine}ANY@{fg text} , @{fg shine}PUBLIC@{fg text} , @{fg shine}CHIP@{fg text} ,    
                             @{fg shine}FAST@{fg text} or @{fg shine}24BIT@{fg text} . Replaces the            
                             driver-reported type instead of adding  
                             to it; @{fg shine}MEMF_PUBLIC@{fg text} is always included.  

@{fg highlight}
  dd RAM:image scsi.device 0 MT 262144
  dd RAM:image scsi.device 0 MT 65024 MEM 24BIT
@{fg text}

@{fg shine}dd INSPECT@{fg text} shows what the driver claims ( @{fg shine}buf mem type:@{fg text} ), and a
transfer started with @{fg shine}MEM=@{fg text} names the type it was told to use on its
@{fg shine}xfer:@{fg text} line.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Forcing_the_command}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Examples}

@endnode

@node Examples "Examples"
@{b}Examples@{ub}

@{fg highlight}
  ; copy 128 blocks from scsi.device unit 1 into a file
  dd scsi.device ram:dump 1 0 128
  
  ; write a file back to scsi.device unit 1
  dd ram:dump scsi.device 1 0 128
  
  ; zero-fill compactflash.device unit 0 (entire disk)
  dd FILL: compactflash.device 0
  
  ; read 2000 blocks at LBA 8388000 from CF unit 0 into a file
  dd compactflash.device ram:chunk 0 8388000 2000
  
  ; device-to-device clone, use keyword form for separate units
  dd cf.device scsi.device US 0 UD 1 START 0 COUNT 1000
  
  ; fully keyword form (always works)
  dd SRC compactflash.device DST ram:dump UNIT 0 START 100 COUNT 200
  
  ; probe a device, print its capabilities, do no I/O
  dd INSPECT compactflash.device UNIT 0
  dd I scsi.device
  
  ; probe and also list the full SupportedCommands set
  dd INSPECT scsi.device VERBOSE
  
  ; force a specific transfer command (no auto-fallback)
  dd uaehf.device NIL: 0 CMD=TD64
  dd scsi.device RAM:dump 0 0 1000 CMD=SCSI
  
  ; different command per side on a device-to-device copy
  dd cf.device scsi.device US 0 UD 0 CS TD64 CD NSCMD
  
  ; request size and buffer memory type
  dd RAM:image scsi.device 0 MT 65024 MEM 24BIT
@{fg text}

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Transfer_size_and_buffer_memory}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Migration_from_dd_1_x}

@endnode

@node Migration_from_dd_1_x "Migration from dd 1.x"
@{b}Migration from dd 1.x@{ub}

dd 2.0 introduced a CLI breaking change. In dd 1.x the unit number
followed each @{fg shine}*.device@{fg text} argument inline. In dd 2.x the positional order
is always @{fg shine}SRC DST [UNIT] [START] [COUNT] [BS]@{fg text} ; the unit moves from
between-SRC-and-DST to after-DST. @{fg shine}FILL:@{fg text} / @{fg shine}RSPEED:@{fg text} / @{fg shine}RWSPEED:@{fg text} are
unchanged.

--- Table ---
  1. Use case
  2. dd 1.x CLI
  3. dd 2.x CLI

  1. Fill device
  2. @{fg shine}dd FILL: scsi.device 0@{fg text}
  3. @{fg shine}dd FILL: scsi.device 0@{fg text} @{i}(unchanged)@{ui}

  1. Fill device with count + block size
  2. @{fg shine}dd FILL: scsi.device 0 0 1048576 512@{fg text}
  3. @{fg shine}dd FILL: scsi.device 0 0 1048576 512@{fg text} @{i}(unchanged)@{ui}

  1. Write file to device
  2. @{fg shine}dd ram:dump scsi.device 1 0 128@{fg text}
  3. @{fg shine}dd ram:dump scsi.device 1 0 128@{fg text} @{i}(unchanged)@{ui}

  1. Read region from device into file
  2. @{fg shine}dd scsi.device 1 ram:dump 0 128@{fg text}
  3. @{fg shine}dd scsi.device ram:dump 1 0 128@{fg text}

  1. Device-to-device copy
  2. @{fg shine}dd cf.device 0 scsi.device 1 0 1000@{fg text}
  3. @{fg shine}dd cf.device scsi.device US 0 UD 1 START 0 COUNT 1000@{fg text}

  1. Help
  2. bespoke help text
  3. @{fg shine}dd ?@{fg text}

--- End Table ---

